Who Is Allowed So far the agent has been open: anyone who could reach port 8791 was local , and local could do everything. That is right on your own machine and wrong on a network. This lesson closes it, in two ways that fit two kinds of caller: people sign in to the web app with an account, and programs (a script, the terminal chat on another machine, CI) send an access token . What you will build The web app with two accounts, ada (an admin) and bo (a member), the agent API behind tokens, and a token for a laptop, a script and a phone, one of which you revoke. About 25 minutes. What changes when it is closed Open (so far) With accounts and tokens Who the caller is local , always The signed-in account, or the token's name Conversations and notes One shared set Each person's own The agent API Anyone who can reach the port A sign-in cookie or an x-bxagents-token header, else 401 Admin pages and routes Everyone Admins only ( 403 to the rest) There are two roles. A member talks to the agent and sees only their own conversations, notes and preferences. An admin can also do what the operator of the app does: see everyone's conversations, manage access tokens, change the agent's own notes and the secrets, see where the agent keeps its data. The terminal on the server and an admin token are admins too. 1. Two accounts Accounts are written in the web gateway. A password is never written in the file: an account names the environment variable that holds it (or carries an already-hashed value; bxAgents hash-password makes one). Replace the gateway file from The Web App with this one: class { function configure() { return { exposes : "webui", path : "/chat", users : [ { username : "ada", passwordEnvVar : "ACME_ADA_PASSWORD", displayName : "Ada Lovelace", admin : true }, { username : "bo", passwordEnvVar : "ACME_BO_PASSWORD", displayName : "Bo Park" } ] }; } } An account whose variable is not set is skipped, with a warning in the webui-auth log: it fails closed on purpose, because an account with an empty password would be far worse than none. 2. Tokens for programs Tokens are made and kept by the agent itself. Turn them on, and let an admin manage them over the API too, with these lines at the end of Agent.yml ( agentApi is the agent's HTTP API, the one you called from curl): agentApi: tokens: true manageTokens: true Rebuild and serve it (Terminal 3), after stopping the old server. The two export lines give the accounts their passwords for this run only; use your own words for a real deployment: cd ~/bxagents-course/acme-support bxAgents build Build succeeded: <anything>/acme-support/.build/app cd ~/bxagents-course/acme-support export ACME_ADA_PASSWORD=ada-demo-pw ACME_BO_PASSWORD=bo-demo-pw bxAgents serve --port=8791 The agent API has stopped being open. A call with no credentials is refused, and the accounts exist: B=http://127.0.0.1:8791/__bxagents curl -s -o /dev/null -w '%{http_code}
' $B/whoami bxAgents users list 401 USERNAME<anything>ROLE<anything>STATUS<anything>NAME ada<anything>admin<anything>active<anything>Ada Lovelace bo<anything>member<anything>active<anything>Bo Park admin: true in the file made ada an admin when her account was created. After that the database keeps the flag, and bxAgents users admin --name=bo or users member --name=bo changes it (the last admin cannot be demoted). Now make a token. It is printed once , and only a hash is stored: bxAgents token create --name=laptop Token for [laptop] (chat, never expires): bxa_<anything> Copy it now: it is not stored and cannot be shown again. A caller sends it in the x-bxagents-token header; ... A token has a scope , chat (talk to the agent, the default) or admin , and may expire ( --expires=90d ). Make one of each, call the API with them, and see what the scope allows. The token's name is who the caller is, so it is also the owner of the conversations and notes it creates: B=http://127.0.0.1:8791/__bxagents ADMIN=$(bxAgents token create --name=desk --scope=admin | grep -o 'bxa_[A-Za-z0-9_-]*') CHAT=$(bxAgents token create --name=phone | grep -o 'bxa_[A-Za-z0-9_-]*') curl -s -H "x-bxagents-token: $ADMIN" $B/whoami echo curl -s -H "x-bxagents-token: $CHAT" $B/whoami echo for T in $CHAT $ADMIN; do curl -s -o /dev/null -w '%{http_code} ' -H "x-bxagents-token: $T" $B/logs; done echo {<anything>"scope":"admin"<anything>"id":"desk"<anything>} {<anything>"scope":"chat"<anything>"id":"phone"<anything>} 403 200 The logs route is for admins: the chat token gets 403 , the admin token 200 . Take a token back, and it stops at once, on the running server. token list shows each token's name, scope, status and when it was last used, never the token itself: B=http://127.0.0.1:8791/__bxagents PHONE=$(bxAgents token create --name=old-phone | grep -o 'bxa_[A-Za-z0-9_-]*') curl -s -o /dev/null -w '%{http_code}
' -H "x-bxagents-token: $PHONE" $B/whoami bxAgents token revoke --name=old-phone curl -s -o /dev/null -w '%{http_code}
' -H "x-bxagents-token: $PHONE" $B/whoami bxAgents token list 200 Revoked [old-phone]. It stops working at once. 401 NAME<anything>SCOPE<anything>STATUS<anything>CREATED<anything>LAST USED<anything>EXPIRES ... old-phone<anything>chat<anything>revoked<anything> ... laptop<anything>chat<anything>active<anything> Use a token with the terminal chat on another machine: bxAgents chat --connect=http://127.0.0.1:8791 --token=<the token> 3. Sign in as a person Open http://127.0.0.1:8791/chat in a browser. You are sent to the sign-in form. Sign in as bo with bo-demo-pw : the chat works, the menu has the Main group only, and an address under the Admin group answers 403 . Sign out (the header) and sign in as ada : the Admin group appears, and the Access page lists the tokens you made (a Tokens tab, because manageTokens is on) and the accounts ( People ). A conversation bo starts is invisible to ada 's own list (an admin can switch to Everyone ), and bo 's notes are bo 's. A run that stops for an approval belongs to whoever started it: ada cannot approve bo 's pending routine from her own session. What to remember Passwords are never in the file , only the name of a variable or a hash. A password in a command line stays in your shell history, which is why bxAgents hash-password --password=... is for throwaway passwords or a hash you paste once. Accounts close the API too. With users , /__bxagents needs the sign-in cookie or a token, whatever agentApi says. The rest of the course therefore sends a token when it calls the API. Tokens are secrets. Shown once, kept as a hash, revocable, optionally expiring. agentApi.tokenEnvVar adds one shared secret that acts as an admin, for a deployment you manage through an environment variable. It is a fixed roster. No self-registration, no password reset and no roles beyond admin and member; for your own identity system see the web app reference . Checkpoint gateways/web.bx has the two accounts and Agent.yml ends with the agentApi block. The tokens are in your data home. kit/resume.sh users-roles-and-tokens restores the project files; rebuild after. Full reference: Users and sign-in , access tokens and token , users and hash-password . Next: Secrets the Agent May Use
